Viral Gastroenteritis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Watery non-bloody diarrhea, Nausea and vomiting, Crampy abdominal pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CIA-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, frequent vomiting and diarrhea with inability to retain fluids.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting a sudden onset of profuse, watery diarrhea and episodic vomiting occurring over the last 6 hours. The patient is currently unable to tolerate any oral intake, posing an imminent risk of significant clinical dehydration and electrolyte imbalance. An urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to evaluate hydration status, rule out acute surgical abdominal pathology (e.g., appendicitis or bowel obstruction), and provide immediate antiemetic stabilization. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period poses an unacceptable risk of metabolic deterioration and potential hospital admission for intravenous rehydration, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't stay off the toilet, and I'm vomiting everything I try to drink. I feel completely drained.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated suddenly 8 hours ago with diffuse and crampy abdominal pain, rapidly followed by frequent watery and non-bloody diarrhea. The patient reports nausea and multiple episodes of non-bilious vomiting. Describes generalized malaise and significant myalgias. Patient notes a mild, self-limiting fever earlier in the evening. Denies hematochezia, severe localized pain, or recent travel history.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to the bathroom and bed due to symptom frequency.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High risk of dehydration if oral rehydration cannot be established.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 112/74 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.6 °C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, but appearing fatigued and mildly pale. Dry mucous membranes and slightly decreased skin turgor noted.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A normal basic metabolic panel would assist in confirming the diagnosis in cases of mild dehydration, but is not acutely indicated for this stable patient.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-distended abdomen with diffuse mild tenderness but an absolute lack of focal peritoneal signs (no guarding, rigidity, or rebound). Hyperactive bowel sounds are audible in all four quadrants. No palpable masses or organomegaly.
Environmental Observations: Residence is secure; patient has ready access to oral rehydration salts.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient attempted to take an over-the-counter anti-diarrheal without success.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Viral Gastroenteritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out definitively by the lack of migratory pain to the right lower quadrant and the absence of localized guarding or rebound tenderness.
Bacterial Enterocolitis - Less likely given the non-bloody nature of the stool and absence of high-grade toxic fever, though warrants follow-up if symptoms persist.
Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out by the presence of hyperactive (rather than absent or tinkling) bowel sounds and the passage of frequent diarrhea.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Dehydration risk living alone)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient's inability to retain fluids due to frequent vomiting and diarrhea necessitated an urgent evaluation to exclude surgical pathology and manage acute dehydration. Clinical assessment confirmed a classic presentation of viral gastroenteritis with mild signs of dehydration. Immediate stabilization with a sublingual antiemetic was required to halt the vomiting cycle and allow for the resumption of oral rehydration. Providing this intervention in the home prevented a likely emergency department presentation for IV fluids, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually stat. Prescribed a short course of oral rehydration salts (ORS). Advised Paracetamol 1000mg strictly 6-hourly for myalgia and fever.
Positive Behavioral Change: To successfully rehydrate without triggering further vomiting, take exactly 5-10ml of oral rehydration solution every 5 minutes using a small spoon rather than drinking from a glass. This "micro-dosing" allows the stomach to process small volumes without triggering the stretch receptors that cause reflex vomiting.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 48 hours if diarrhea persists or if unable to maintain hydration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe, constant abdominal pain, start passing bloody stools, become profoundly dizzy upon standing, or stop passing urine for more than 8 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; viral gastroenteritis is self-limiting but requires strict fluid management.
Clinical Course: Vomiting typically resolves within 12-24 hours. Diarrhea may persist for 3-5 days. Spontaneous resolution is expected as the viral load clears.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if you develop a high-grade fever over 39°C or if vomiting returns despite the medication.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A virus is irritating your gut lining, causing it to flush out water (diarrhea) and reject intake (vomiting). The wafer I gave you blocks the vomit signal in your brain so you can start putting back the water and salts you've lost.
Actionable Steps: Avoid plain water; use only oral rehydration solutions to ensure electrolyte balance. Maintain strict hand hygiene to prevent spreading the virus to others.

